      "text": "1. To find the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of 1/7, we first determine the decimal representation. Using long division, we find that 1/7 = 0.142857142857.... This is a repeating decimal, and 0.142857 is the repeating cycle. \n2. Since the repeating cycle of 0.142857 has a length of 6 digits, we can find the 100th digit by determining its position within the repeating cycle. To do this, we divide 100 by 6 and get a remainder of 4. \n3. This means that the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating cycle, which is 8.\n4. So, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of 1/7 is \\boxed{8}.",
