    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we start by finding the decimal expansion of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\).\n\nFirst, we perform long division of 1 by 7:\n\n1. 1 divided by 7 is 0, so we write down 0 and then consider the next digit, which is 10 by interpreting it as 10 units of 7. This gives us 0. $\\overline{142857}$\nSo, the decimals expansion of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\( 0.\\overline{142857} \\). We see that each 7 repeating blocks of digits follows.\n\nThe length of each repeating block is 6.\n\nTo find the 100th digit, we need to determine where 100 falls within the repeating block. We use modulo arithmetic for this, recognizing that:\n\n\\[ 100 \\div 6 \\approx 16. \\]\n\nSince 16 is a whole number, the decimal modulo 100 will be the same as the modulo value, which is the remainder of the division \\( 100 \\mod 6 \\):\n\n\\[ 100 \\mod 6 = 4 \\]\n\nThis tells us that the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit within the repeating hexadecimal sequence (blocks). The repeating block is 142857, so the 4th digit is 2.\n\nThis means that at the 100th place in the decimal expansion of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), the digit is 2. Therefore the 100th digit after the decimal point of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\boxed{2}.",
